"**Argumend map: Does rent control make housing less affordable in the long run?**
The map's claim: Rent control policies reduce housing affordability in the long run by discouraging new construction and reducing housing supply.

The largest share of this thread (4 of 8 turns) is arguing about **Supply Effects**. 1 of 8 turns was not an argument about the topic. Some turns argue about different sections of the map as if they were one disagreement. A key term is being used to mean different things.

**Pattern:** Mixed disagreement.

**The crux for this section:** The Rental Supply Response Test. Measure both new housing starts and withdrawals of existing rental units under rent stabilization. Stable construction alone does not establish stable rental supply: conversions to owner occupancy can remove rentals even when new buildings are exempt.

**Strongest evidence in this section:**
- Supports the map's claim: Stanford Study: San Francisco Rent Control Reduced Rental Supply by 15%. Diamond, McQuade & Qian, American Economic Review, 109(9): 3365-94 (2019)
- Cuts against the map's claim: Cambridge Decontrol Raised Property Values via Spillovers, Not Mainly New Construction (Autor, Palmer & Pathak 2014). Autor, Palmer & Pathak, Journal of Political Economy, 122(3): 661-717 (2014)

**Cruxes this thread touched:** The Rental Supply Response Test | The Bridge-or-Delay Test
**Never reached:** Neighborhood stability and access for new renters

Full map: https://argumend.org/topics/rent-control-effectiveness"
